.TH CCOL_SELECTABLE_FROM_CIRCQ 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_selectable_from_circq \- build a ccol_selectable from a ccol_circular_queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_selectable_from_circq(q, dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Constructs a ccol_selectable tagged-union value of type ccol_selectable_circq
wrapping the given ccol_circular_queue pointer with the specified direction.

  ccol_select_read; wait for at least one message to arrive (read-wait)
  ccol_select_write; wait until the queue has room for a send (write-wait)

This is a macro that expands to a compound literal; it has no run-time
overhead beyond loading the pointer.

ccol_select() only reports readiness for either direction; it never
consumes or reserves anything itself.  The caller must subsequently call
ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() (read-direction win) or ccol_circq_try_send_zc()
(write-direction win) itself, since nothing is claimed or reserved by
ccol_select() (TOCTOU, same as POSIX select(2)).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
q    ccol_circular_queue pointer to wrap.  Passing NULL produces a selectable
     that ccol_select() will reject with ccol_invalid_args.
dir  ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_selectable value (not a pointer).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_circular_queue *q0 = ccol_circular_queue_create(8, NULL);
ccol_circular_queue *q1 = ccol_circular_queue_create(8, NULL);

size_t idx;
/* Read-wait on two queues: */
ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read));
/* on ccol_success, claim the message via ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() on
   whichever of q0/q1 idx points at */

/* Mixed: wait until q0 has room to send or q1 has a message: */
ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_write),
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read));
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_select (3),
.BR ccol_select_va (3),
